{
  "dom_id": "question-166822",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "In preparation for his entire week's work, the painter purchased $8\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ liters of thinner. Of this amount, he used $7\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ liters. How much thinner did he have left? Express your answer as a mixed fraction.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The painter had$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$liters of thinner left over.",
  "worked_solution": "The painter had $(8\\,\\frac{5}{6}-7\\,\\frac{2}{3})$ liters of thinner left over.\n\nWriting $8\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n8\\,\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{8\\times 6+5}{6}=\\frac{53}{6}.\n$$\n\nWriting $7\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n7\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{7\\times 3+2}{3}=\\frac{23}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{53}{6}-\\frac{23}{3}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $6.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{23}{3}$ over a denominator of $6,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{23}{3}=\\frac{23\\times 2}{3\\times 2}=\\frac{46}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{53}{6}-\\frac{46}{6}=\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{6}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the painter had $1\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ liters of thinner left over.",
